course: dictionary
lesson: 10211
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many English words have more than one meaning, and tissue is a good example. You can tell which meaning is meant by reading the words around it. «Catch your sneeze in a tissue, then bin it and wash your hands.» Here a tissue is a soft piece of paper, because you use it to wipe your nose.
explain: The same word can mean something different in science. «Muscle tissue» is a group of similar cells that work together in a living thing. The clue is the word before it: muscle tells you this is about the body, not paper.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A tissue can be a soft, thin piece of paper for wiping or blowing your nose.
fact: In biology, a tissue is a group of similar cells that work together in a living thing.
fact: Tissue paper is very thin paper used for wrapping presents or crafts.
fact: Cells make tissues, and tissues make organs, such as the heart and the skin.
fact: «Scar tissue» has no hair or sweat glands, and scars fade over years but rarely vanish completely.
exercise: «A sneeze can spray droplets, so catch it in a tissue, then bin it and wash your hands.» What is a tissue in this sentence? | A kind of muscle | Very thin paper for wrapping presents | *A soft, thin piece of paper for your nose | A group of cells in a living thing
exercise: «Muscle tissue» is a phrase from science. What does tissue mean here? | A thin cloth for cleaning | A box of soft paper | Thin paper for crafts | *A group of similar cells that work together
exercise: «Tissue paper» is used for wrapping presents. What does tissue mean in this phrase? | *Very thin paper | A living cell that is very small | A soft paper for blowing your nose | A group of organs
test: «When a baby tooth comes out and the gap bleeds, biting on clean gauze or tissue for a few minutes stops it.» Which meaning of tissue fits here? | A group of cells in the body | Something from a muscle | Thin paper for wrapping | *A soft pad of paper or cloth to press on
test: Which phrase uses tissue to mean a group of cells that work together? | Tissue for your nose | *Muscle tissue | Tissue paper | A box of tissues
exam: In «Watercolour can sometimes be lifted with a tissue», what does tissue mean? | A box of paper for crafts | A group of cells in a living thing | *A soft paper you press on to take paint away | Thin paper for wrapping presents
